EXT. LIFE RAFT - OPEN OCEAN - DAY

A bright, flat sea. A canopy of orange rubber snaps in the breeze. Three people sit around a pile of ration tins.

ORLA FITZGIBBON, 30s, sunburned, trims her fingernails with a pocketknife. JASPER TOLENTINO, 20s, lies back with a hat over his face. PETRA HALVORSEN, 40s, tends a small fishing line looped over the side.

ORLA
Jasper, you awake?

JASPER
(under the hat)
No.

ORLA
Petra says we've got water for nine days.

PETRA
Ten, if Jasper stops sweating.

JASPER
I'm not sweating. The sun's sweating.

The sea is calm. The three of them breathe in the heat. Petra checks the line. Orla looks at the horizon and sighs, content.

ORLA
You know, this isn't so bad.

PETRA
I was just thinking the same.

JASPER
(sitting up)
Me too, honestly.

ORLA ^
It's almost nice.

PETRA
It's almost nice.

Petra pulls the line. Something heavy answers. She grins and heaves. Orla grabs her waist. Jasper scrambles to help. A fin breaks the surface. A big one. Then another.

JASPER
Petra, let go.

PETRA
It's a fish!

ORLA
Petra, it's not a fish!

The raft tilts. A dark shape rolls under them, rubbing against the hull. Orla's grin dies.

PETRA
(whispering)
Jasper, get the oar.

JASPER
Petra, I'm getting it. Orla, hold the line.

ORLA
I'm holding. I'm holding!

The shape surfaces beside the raft. A shark's flat, indifferent eye regards them.
